WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.050
RPX tests robot perception as the same physical scene changes.

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:13.530
Modular pipelines, foundation models, and agentic systems all depend on perception.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:19.520
Perception guides planning and control. But
each action changes what the robot sees next.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:25.080
Separate benchmarks make it difficult to compare
perception backbones across changes in the same scene.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:31.380
Perception should stay accurate as scenes change.
RPX measures both quality and phase robustness.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:36.990
Our handheld rig records color, depth, stereo views, and camera poses.

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:44.180
A person manipulates the objects. We capture Clutter,
Interaction, Clean, and simultaneous egocentric view.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:50.100
One protocol spans a hundred multi-object
scenes, seventy daily objects, and twenty settings.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:55.840
Multiple views and sensing modalities show how
those same scenes change through manipulation.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:01.980
The settings vary in surfaces, lighting, and
background, across indoor and outdoor spaces.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:07.730
Each daily object also has its own single-object
scene, recorded from multiple viewpoints.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.250
Each multi-object scene has all four captures.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:14.510
Single-object scenes provide isolated references.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:19.090
Ego shares images, masks, and questionnaires without depth.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:22.330
These paired measurements support multiple tasks.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:27.110
Ego contributes to tracking and both question-answering tasks.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:31.480
Object questions describe use, color, and material.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.050
Reference images support in-context grounding.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:40.380
Six tasks span image and video depth, tracking, relative pose, and question answering.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:45.690
Stable can still mean wrong. A strong average can hide a weak phase.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:50.670
For each model: scenes, phases, and task metrics.

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:53.940
Phase and difficulty slices feed our two scores.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:59.700
Phi compares phases across matched scenes.
Higher Phi means a smaller phase effect.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.390
JEDI combines normalized metrics, then reports quality in the model's weakest phase.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:12.080
Models that land in the golden region are likely high
quality, along with having strong phase robustness.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:18.350
These two video-depth models share the same
average quality, but their phase profiles differ.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:24.110
Interaction is DA3's weakest phase. Worst-phase
quality reveals what the average hides.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:30.570
Across forty-nine pretrained models tested zero-shot,
depth and tracking show strong phase effects.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:34.970
Relative pose changes less, comparing Clutter and Clean performance.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:39.960
Question-answering quality is low, and visual context changes the model rankings.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:43.980
None of the sixty-five evaluations reaches this golden region.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:48.280
Bring your own model. Adapters handle its inputs and outputs.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:52.800
The RPX Toolkit is designed to grow with new tasks and metrics.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.840
Scan to see the code that made it all work.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.360
The full dataset follows upon acceptance.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:02:59.900
Thank you very much for watching!

